WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:03.060
SPEAKER: More and more companies
are looking to unlock business

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:04.980
insights from their data.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.280
But it can be hard to scalably
ingest, store, and analyze

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.770
that data as it rapidly grows.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.260
Google's enterprise data
warehouse, BigQuery,

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.990
was designed to make
large-scale data analysis

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:17.670
accessible to everyone.

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:19.830
In this series,
we'll show you how

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:22.170
BigQuery can help you
get valuable insights

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:23.790
from your data with ease.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:27.480
Ask questions and get answers
on "BigQuery Spotlight."

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:35.740
If you're a developer,
data analyst,

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:37.240
or just about
anyone else, you're

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:38.830
probably working with data.

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:41.050
If your business has
small amounts of data,

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:43.060
you might be able to
store it in a spreadsheet.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:45.490
But as your amount of
data grows to gigabytes,

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:47.650
terabytes, or even
petabytes, you

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:50.620
start to need a more efficient
system like a data warehouse.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:54.310
That's because all that data
isn't very useful unless you

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:55.930
have a way to analyze it.

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:58.000
Traditionally,
larger sets of data

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.700
mean longer times between
asking your questions

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.440
and getting answers.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:05.740
Have you ever needed to wait
hours or days for an analytics

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:06.860
report to be run?

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:10.210
BigQuery was designed to
handle massive amounts of data,

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:14.050
such as log data from thousands
of retail systems or IoT data

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:16.610
from millions of vehicle
sensors across the globe.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:19.880
It's a fully managed and
serverless data warehouse

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:23.080
which empowers you to focus on
analytics instead of managing

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:25.000
infrastructure.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.750
By design, BigQuery helps you
avoid the data silo problem

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:30.730
which happens when you
have individual teams

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:34.390
in your company having their
own independent data marts.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:37.600
This can create significant
friction between analyzing data

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.030
across teams and
cause challenges

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:41.290
with data version control.

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:44.440
Thanks to the integration with
Google Cloud's native identity

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:47.410
and access management, you
can assign read or write

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:51.010
permissions to specific
users, groups, or projects,

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:53.140
and keep your
sensitive data secure,

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:55.510
all while still
collaborating across teams.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:58.960
Working with data in BigQuery
involves three primary parts--

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:02.170
storage, ingestion,
and querying.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:04.990
Google handles running
everything else.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:07.610
BigQuery is a fully
managed service,

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:10.240
which means you don't need to
set up or install anything.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.340
And you don't require a
database administrator.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.730
You can simply log into
your Google Cloud project

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:18.050
from a browser and get started.

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:20.440
First, let's talk about
BigQuery's storage.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.930
Data is stored in a
structured table, which

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:26.470
means that you can use standard
SQL for easy querying and data

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:27.580
analysis.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.080
For example, let's
say that you have

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:32.350
some data that represents the
sales for each of your stores

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:33.850
in the last year.

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:36.440
You could probably use a
smaller database for that.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.230
But what if you have
thousands of stores?

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:41.110
And what if you want revenue
broken up by product SKU

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:43.780
or by region per time period?

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.810
BigQuery is perfect
for big data because we

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:49.780
manage all that storage
and the scaling operations

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:51.940
automatically for you.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:53.680
Of course, storing
the data doesn't

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.913
matter if you can't get into
BigQuery in the first place.

00:02:56.913 --> 00:02:58.330
Thankfully, there
are lots of ways

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:00.430
to do that, as
BigQuery is integrated

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:03.710
with the rest of the data
analytics platform from Google.

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:06.970
You can upload data from Cloud
Storage and just streaming

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:10.090
data from Cloud Dataflow,
build an ETL pipeline using

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:13.990
Cloud data fusion, import data
from a variety of file formats,

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:16.420
or use a combination
of all of these.

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:19.050
We'll go over these and
more in future episodes

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:20.620
so that you can
decide which setup

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:22.330
is right for your data sets.

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:24.010
Once your data is
in BigQuery, you're

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:25.870
ready to start answering
those questions.

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:28.480
BigQuery supports the same
Structured Query Language,

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:30.760
or SQL, that you may
be familiar with if you

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:33.250
worked with ANSI-compliant
relational databases

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:34.510
in the past.

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:37.690
We'll go over multiple ways
to work with data in BigQuery

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:40.690
so that you can choose what's
best for your use case.

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:43.630
Plus, you'll be able to share
access with other users,

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:46.960
so they can derive insights
from your data, too.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.000
Want to skip straight
to analyzing some data?

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.610
You can bypass the
ingestion and storage steps

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:54.582
by analyzing the BigQuery
public data sets.

00:03:54.582 --> 00:03:57.040
These are third-party data sets
which have been made public

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.110
for anyone to query against.

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:01.360
Google handles all the
storage so that you

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:03.550
can focus on figuring
out answers to questions,

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:05.500
like, how does the
weather in New York City

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:07.600
affect demand for taxicabs?

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:10.510
In this series, we'll walk you
through the play-by-play, some

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:12.190
of the most common
tasks in BigQuery,

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:15.732
and share some best practices
to help you up your game.

00:04:15.732 --> 00:04:18.190
But if you want to get started
right now, click on the link

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:20.440
below, and start with
the BigQuery sandbox.

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:23.250
The sandbox gives you a free
environment to trial BigQuery.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:25.250
And you can get started
by analyzing public data

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:26.620
sets immediately.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:28.960
Look out for our next episode
of "BigQuery Spotlight."

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.590
And remember, stay curious.

